ure how to form a hand.\n\nNow, considering the melds I already have: F3 pung and B4-B5-B6 chi. My current hand is 8 tiles. To win, I need to have a 14th tile that completes a hand with four melds and a pair. Let's see if any of the playable tiles can help me form a pair or another meld.\n\nLooking at the remaining tiles, maybe if I can form another pung or chi. For example, if I have W2 and W4, but no W3. If I have W4 and W5, maybe W4-W5-W6, but no W6. Similarly, T4 and T9 are terminals. B3 is a single tile.\n\nWait, my current hand has W2, W4, W5, W9, T4, T9, B3, F1. Let me check if any of these can form a pair or another meld. For example, if I have a pair of T9s, but I only have one. Similarly, W2 is a single. Maybe I need to discard a tile that's not part of any possible meld or pair.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, the counts for each tile might help. For example, B3 has 4 remaining. If I discard B3, maybe someone else can take it. But how does that help me?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should look for a way to form a pair. For example, if I can form a pair with W5 and another W5. But I only have one W5. Or maybe a pair with T4 and T9? No, they are different suits.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a seven pairs hand? Let me see. If I can make seven pairs. But with my current tiles, I have only one of each. So that's not possible unless I draw another tile. But I'm already at 8 tiles, so adding a 9th would make 9, but to win, I need to have 14 tiles. Wait, no, I have 8 hand tiles plus the melds. Wait, the melds are two pungs (3+3 tiles), so total tiles in hand would be 8 + 6 = 14. Wait, but I have 8 tiles in hand and two melds. Wait, the melds are [F3x3] and [B4-B5-B6], which is 3+3=6 tiles. So my hand has 8 tiles, which is correct for a winning hand (14 total tiles: 6 melds + 8 hand tiles? Wait, no. Wait, the total number of tiles in a winning hand is 14. If I have two melds (3+3=6 tiles), then the remaining 8 tiles in hand must form four melds and a pair. Wait, no. Wa