 B4-B5-B6, so maybe T4 is part of another sequence. But I don't have T3 or T5. So T4 is a single tile. Discarding it could be okay.\n\nW4: I have two W4s. Discarding one W4 would leave me with one W4. If I can form a pair or a Pung with the remaining, but I have only one. So maybe it's a single. But if I keep both, maybe I can form a Pung if another W4 is drawn. But since the unshown tiles have W4:3, so there are 3 left. So the chance of getting another is possible. But since I have two, maybe keeping one is better.\n\nT6: I have two T6s. Discarding one would leave one. T6 is a middle tile. If I have T5 or T7, I could form a Chi, but I don't. So it's a single. However, if I discard T6, others might need it for a Chi. But again, depends on their hands.\n\nF1: I have one F1. Discarding it would be a single. Since I have a Pung of F3, maybe F1 is not part of that. But if I discard F1, maybe others can use it for a Pung if they have F1s. But there are two F1s left (unshown F1:2). So maybe not critical.\n\nB6: I have one B6. Wait, I already have a Chi of B4-B5-B6. So the B6 is part of that Chi. So if I discard B6, it's part of the meld. But since the Chi is already formed, discarding B6 is allowed. However, since it's part of a meld, maybe it's better not to discard it. Wait, no, once the meld is formed, the tiles are considered part of the meld, so discarding one from the meld would be possible. But in this case, the Chi is already in the melds, so maybe B6 is already part of that. So discarding B6 is possible. However, if I discard B6, others might use it for a Chi if they have B4 or B5. But since the unshown B5 is 0, so B5 is already all used up. So B6 is part of the Chi. If I discard B6, maybe others can't use it because B5 is gone.\n\nNow, considering the game state. The next player has played W3, F4, etc. The player across has melds of B3 and W5. The previous player has some melds. The key is to avoid giving others a chance to win when they discard a tile I discard