4, which is not a terminal. \n\nAlternatively, if there's a different pair. For example, if I discard a tile to form a different pair. Let's see:\n\nIf I discard W3, the remaining tiles are W4 (2), T6 (1), T7 (2), T8 (1), B8 (1). The pair is W4. \n\nIf I discard W4, the remaining tiles are W3 (1), T6 (1), T7 (2), T8 (1), B8 (1). The pair is T7 (2). \n\nIf I discard T8, remaining tiles: W3 (1), W4 (2), T6 (1), T7 (2), B8 (1). Pair is W4. \n\nIf I discard B8, remaining tiles: W3 (1), W4 (2), T6 (1), T7 (2), T8 (1). Pair is W4. \n\nIf I discard T6, remaining tiles: W3 (1), W4 (2), T7 (2), T8 (1), B8 (1). Pair is T7. \n\nIf I discard T7, remaining tiles: W3 (1), W4 (2), T6 (1), T7 (1), T8 (1), B8 (1). Pair is W4. \n\nNone of these pairs are terminals or honors. So no Outside Hand. \n\nIs there any other pattern? Maybe the hand has a concealed kong (4), and the All Chows pattern? But All Chows requires four chows and a pair. Here, there's a kong and two chows. So no. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the hand has a concealed kong and a pair of W4, which is a simple tile. So All Simples is 2 points. Also, the hand has a concealed kong (4 points) and a Pung of Honors (1 point). Total 7. Still not enough. \n\nWait, maybe there's a mistake in the melds. Let me check the melds again. The user's melds are [[F3, F3, F3, F3], [B4, B5, B6]]. The [B4, B5, B6] is a chi, which is a chow. So that's correct. \n\nHmm. This suggests that none of the plays would result in a valid hand with 8 points. But that can't be right. Maybe I'm missing a pattern. \n\nWait, what if the hand is a Seven Pairs? No, the hand has six tiles, but Seven Pairs requires seven pairs. Not applicable here. \n\nWhat about Chicken Hand? A hand that would otherwise be zero fans. But the current hand has 7 points, which is more than zero. So Chicken Hand is not applicable. \n\nIs there a possibility that the hand has multiple instances of a pattern? For example, the concealed kong is 4 points, and the Pung of Honors is 1 point, and All Simples is 2 points. Total 7. But maybe there's another 1-point pattern. \n\nFor example, the hand has a concealed kong (4), Pung of Honors (1), All Simples (2), and maybe One Voided Suit (1 point). The hand has Dots and Characters suits. So One Voided Suit is applicable (only two suits). That's 1 point. Total 4+1+2+1=8 points. \n\nYes! That's 8 points. So the hand would be valid. \n\nSo the total points would be:\n\n- Concealed Kong (4 points)\n\n- Pung of Honors (1 point)\n\n- All Simples (2 points)\n\n- One Voided Suit (1 point)\n\nTotal 8 points. \n\nThat meets the minimum requirement. \n\nTherefore, any of the plays that result in this hand would be valid. \n\nSo the valid actions are Play W3, W4, T8, B8, T6, T7. But we need to choose the one that is safest to discard. \n\nAs previously discussed, discarding T7 is the safest because it has 4 remaining in the wall. Discarding B8 or T6 is risky because they have only 1 left. So the best choice is to discard T7, which is Play T7. \n\nAlternatively, discarding W4 (2 remaining) is also safer than B8 or T6. \n\nBut let's check which discard leads to the same total points. All of them do. So the best choice is to discard the tile that is least useful to others. Since T7 is a middle tile (not terminal), and there are 4 left, it's the safest. \n\nTherefore, the best action is Play T7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding T7 allows forming a valid hand with 8 points by combining a concealed kong, Pung of Honors, All Simples, and One Voided Suit. It is the safest discard due to high remaining tiles.]\nReason: [Discarding T7 (4 remaining) minimizes risk while enabling a winning hand with sufficient points.]\nAnswer: Play T7"}
